Align the interpreter stack and its frames to the POINTER, not to 8 and 4.

Two places, one cause, and the first of them says what it is doing out loud:

    union {
        uint64 __make_it_8_byte_aligned_;
        uint8  bottom[1];
    } wasm_stack_u;

Eight is the pointer's alignment on the targets WAMR was written for. A frame is
placed at `bottom` and its FIRST field is a pointer -- WASMInterpFrame::prev_frame,
at offset 0 -- so on a target where a pointer wants 16 the very first
`frame->prev_frame = prev_frame` stores across an unaligned boundary. Adding a
`void *` member to the union rather than changing the existing one keeps every
other target byte-identical.

The second is wasm_interp_interp_frame_size, which ends `return align_uint(frame_size, 4)`.
Frames are handed out by bumping exec_env->wasm_stack.top by exactly that size, so
a 4-aligned size leaves the NEXT frame unaligned however well the base is placed.
Rounding to sizeof(void *) is a no-op where a pointer is 4 or 8 bytes.

MEASURED, not reasoned. A static-assert probe over the real struct layouts put the
fault exactly here: offsetof(WASMInterpFrame, prev_frame) == 0 passes,
offsetof(WASMInterpFrame, lp) % 16 == 0 passes, and
offsetof(WASMExecEnv, wasm_stack_u) % 16 == 0 FAILS -- it is 8. The faulting
instruction the trap named was `stc s5, 0x0(s2)`, a capability store at offset 0
of a frame, which is that field and nothing else.

Same root as patch 0002: a constant that encodes the pointer's width without
saying so. There it was `UINTPTR_MAX == UINT64_MAX`; here it is the literals 8
and 4.

diff --git i/core/iwasm/common/wasm_exec_env.h w/core/iwasm/common/wasm_exec_env.h
index 5d80312..ea5e9e3 100644
--- i/core/iwasm/common/wasm_exec_env.h
+++ w/core/iwasm/common/wasm_exec_env.h
@@ -168,6 +168,14 @@ typedef struct WASMExecEnv {
     /* The WASM stack of current thread */
     union {
         uint64 __make_it_8_byte_aligned_;
+        /* A frame starts at bottom and its FIRST field is a pointer
+           (WASMInterpFrame::prev_frame, at offset 0), so this union must carry
+           the pointer's alignment and not a fixed 8. On a target where a pointer
+           is wider than uint64 the name above stops describing what is needed,
+           and the first `frame->prev_frame = ...` stores across an unaligned
+           boundary. Adding the member rather than changing the one above keeps
+           every existing target byte-identical. */
+        void *__make_it_pointer_aligned_;
         /* The WASM stack. */
         uint8 bottom[1];
     } wasm_stack_u;
diff --git i/core/iwasm/interpreter/wasm_interp.h w/core/iwasm/interpreter/wasm_interp.h
index 1416405..8f774ea 100644
--- i/core/iwasm/interpreter/wasm_interp.h
+++ w/core/iwasm/interpreter/wasm_interp.h
@@ -98,7 +98,12 @@ wasm_interp_interp_frame_size(unsigned all_cell_num)
 #else
     frame_size = (uint32)offsetof(WASMInterpFrame, operand) + all_cell_num * 4;
 #endif
-    return align_uint(frame_size, 4);
+    /* Aligned to the POINTER, not to 4. Frames are handed out by bumping
+       exec_env->wasm_stack.top by exactly this size, so a size that is only
+       4-aligned leaves the NEXT frame unaligned however well the base is placed,
+       and a frame's first field is a pointer (prev_frame, at offset 0). On a
+       target where a pointer is 4 or 8 bytes this changes nothing. */
+    return align_uint(frame_size, (unsigned)sizeof(void *));
 }
 
 void
